  "year": "1975",
  "subtitle": "The first microprocessor control panel on a household appliance — cooking becomes programming",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Amana Touchmatic Radarange (RR-6, 1975) is widely credited as the first microprocessor-controlled digital control panel on an ordinary home appliance, where a solid-state keypad and display replaced the mechanical dials and knobs of every previous oven. The interaction model is the moment a computer 'brains' interface entered the kitchen: pressing a membrane keypad to key in cook time, defrost-by-time, temperature, and staged/held programs, with a bright digital clock and beeps — programming a machine to transform a physical process. The museum has TNC 110 (machine-tool control), the Tektronix 7854 (lab instrument), and the Buick Riviera GCC (automotive dashboard), but no 'brains-replace-the-dial' home appliance; the Touchmatic is the ur-example of that larger 1970s boundary crossing, preserved in collections (e.g., a 1978 RR5-6 at the Science Museum Group).",
  "search_terms": [
    "Amana Touchmatic Radarange",
    "first microprocessor microwave",
    "RR-6 1975 digital control",
    "first microprocessor control panel home appliance",
    "Amana Radarange touchmatic history"
  ],
  "research": {
    "title": "Amana Touchmatic Radarange (1975)",
    "year": "1975",
    "by": "Amana Refrigeration, Inc. (Amana, Iowa; a Raytheon subsidiary)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Home Appliance", "Microprocessor", "Keypad", "Display", "Consumer"],
    "overview": "The Amana Radarange was the home microwave oven line of Amana Refrigeration, Inc., of Amana, Iowa (a Raytheon subsidiary since 1965). Its early 1970s models (RR-4, RR-4D) used analog timers and mechanical knobs. In 1974 Amana introduced automatic defrost on the RR-4D; then, according to the standard account (including Wikipedia's Microwave oven article), Amana was the first to offer a microprocessor-controlled digital control panel in 1975 with the RR-6 model — the 'Touchmatic' Radarange. The Touchmatic replaced the dials and switches with a solid-state microprocessor, a digital touch keypad, and a bright digital clock/display, letting the user key in cook times, defrost cycles, and (on later models) staged cooking and temperatures. The model line shipped through the late 1970s and early 1980s, and a 1978 RR5-6 unit is held by the Science Museum Group (co8062000).",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The brains-replace-the-dial moment",
        "text": "Until 1975 a microwave user operated a mechanical timer and knob. The Touchmatic replaced that entire layer with a microprocessor and a digit keypad: the user pressed keys to set a cooking time, a defrost time, or a sequence, with the digital display counting down and the oven beeping when done. This is a genuinely different interaction — instead of turning a dial until it pointed at a number, the user 'wrote' a short program (time, power, stage) that the machine executed against the physical process of heating food. The microwave became the first computing appliance most households ever owned."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Timing, staged cooking, and the digital ritual",
        "text": "Microprocessor control allowed multi-stage operations that a mechanical timer could not express: defrost-then-cook, timed hold, and (in later Touchmatic models) temperature probes. The user composes a sequence on the membrane keypad, the display confirms it, and the magnetron runs through the program. The interface is a miniature command-line to a heat engine, wrapped in consumer packaging."
      },
      {
        "heading": "In-window availability and museum survival",
        "text": "Though the RR-6 debuted in 1975, the Touchmatic line was sold throughout the 1976-1992 window and a 1978 RR5-6 is preserved by the Science Museum Group in London. The 1971-74 analog predecessors (RR-4/RR-4D) are preserved by the Smithsonian (nmah_214291) and The Henry Ford, which bracket the transition this artifact marks — from dial-and-knob to keypad-and-display."
      }
    ],
